    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d469">

                <pb facs="0394" n="386" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg386"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">394.1153 Feiting/5: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-01T16:00:00+08:00">December 1, 1937—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 1—7 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1054. My 1048, November 30, 9 p.m. The launch has been returned by the Japanese
                    with the flag restored. The written report made to <pb facs="0395" n="387"
                        xml:id="pg387"/> me by the American company states that the Japanese sailors
                    lowered the American flag and tossed it to the crew of an adjacent launch. The
                    details required by your 593, November 30, 7 p.m. will follow later.<anchor
                        n="21" xml:id="d469-f1"/></p>
                <p>Repeated to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d469-f1" n="21" xml:id="d469-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram No. 1055, December 1, 5 p.m., not printed. The Consul General reported
                    that after careful investigation he considered that the sale of the launch to
                    the American company was made in good faith. (394.1153 Feiting/6)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
